# id, name, latin, bp3d-name pattern (regex on side-less name), group, location, origin, insertion, nerve, action, blood, clinical
M=[]
def m(*a): M.append(a)
UA='upper-limb'; LL='lower-limb'
# ---------------- shoulder and arm ----------------
m('supraspinatus','Supraspinatus','Musculus supraspinatus',r'supraspinatus','shoulder','Supraspinous fossa of the scapula, under the acromion.','Supraspinous fossa of the scapula.','Superior facet of the greater tubercle of the humerus.','Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6).','Starts abduction of the arm (first 15 degrees) and holds the humeral head in the shoulder socket.','Suprascapular and dorsal scapular arteries.','The most commonly torn rotator cuff muscle. A painful arc of movement between 60 and 120 degrees of abduction suggests a tendon problem under the acromion.')
m('infraspinatus','Infraspinatus','Musculus infraspinatus',r'infraspinatus( muscle)?','shoulder','Infraspinous fossa on the back of the scapula.','Infraspinous fossa of the scapula.','Middle facet of the greater tubercle of the humerus.','Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6).','Rotates the arm outward (lateral rotation) and stabilises the shoulder joint.','Suprascapular and circumflex scapular arteries.','Part of the rotator cuff. Weakness of outward rotation suggests a cuff tear or injury of the suprascapular nerve.')
m('teres-minor','Teres minor','Musculus teres minor',r'teres minor','shoulder','Back of the shoulder, below infraspinatus.','Upper two-thirds of the lateral border of the scapula.','Inferior facet of the greater tubercle of the humerus.','Axillary nerve (C5, C6).','Rotates the arm outward and holds the humeral head in the socket.','Circumflex scapular artery.','Part of the rotator cuff. Axillary nerve damage (for example after a shoulder dislocation) weakens it together with deltoid.')
m('teres-major','Teres major','Musculus teres major',r'teres major','shoulder','Back of the shoulder, below teres minor.','Inferior angle and lower lateral border of the scapula.','Medial lip of the bicipital groove of the humerus.','Lower subscapular nerve (C5, C6).','Rotates the arm inward, adducts it and extends it from a flexed position.','Circumflex scapular artery.','Forms the lower border of the quadrangular space. It is not part of the rotator cuff.')
m('subscapularis','Subscapularis','Musculus subscapularis',r'subscapularis','shoulder','Front of the scapula, forming the back wall of the armpit.','Subscapular fossa of the scapula.','Lesser tubercle of the humerus.','Upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5, C6, C7).','Rotates the arm inward and stabilises the front of the shoulder joint.','Subscapular artery and suprascapular artery.','The strongest rotator cuff muscle. A positive "lift-off" test suggests a tear.')
m('coracobrachialis','Coracobrachialis','Musculus coracobrachialis',r'coracobrachialis','arm','Upper inner arm, in the front compartment.','Tip of the coracoid process of the scapula.','Middle of the medial surface of the humerus.','Musculocutaneous nerve (C5, C6, C7).','Flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder.','Brachial artery.','The musculocutaneous nerve pierces it, which is a landmark when finding the nerve.')
m('brachialis','Brachialis','Musculus brachialis',r'brachialis','arm','Deep to biceps brachii in the front of the arm.','Distal half of the front of the humerus.','Ulnar tuberosity and coronoid process of the ulna.','Musculocutaneous nerve (C5, C6) and a small branch of the radial nerve.','The main flexor of the elbow, in every position of the forearm.','Brachial and radial recurrent arteries.','A supracondylar fracture of the humerus can injure the brachial artery and the nerves close to this muscle.')
m('anconeus','Anconeus','Musculus anconeus',r'anconeus','arm','Small triangular muscle behind the elbow.','Lateral epicondyle of the humerus (back).','Lateral side of the olecranon and the upper back of the ulna.','Radial nerve (C7, C8, T1).','Helps triceps extend the elbow and steadies the elbow joint.','Posterior interosseous and middle collateral arteries.','Can be involved in lateral elbow pain together with the extensor tendons.')
m('latissimus-dorsi','Latissimus dorsi','Musculus latissimus dorsi',r'latissimus dorsi','shoulder','Large flat muscle of the back and lower chest wall.','Spinous processes of T7 to L5 and the sacrum (thoracolumbar fascia), the back of the iliac crest, lower three or four ribs.','Floor of the bicipital groove of the humerus.','Thoracodorsal nerve (C6, C7, C8).','Extends, adducts and rotates the arm inward. It is used when climbing or pulling the body up.','Thoracodorsal artery and posterior intercostal arteries.','The muscle is used as a flap in breast reconstruction. Damage to the thoracodorsal nerve in axillary surgery weakens pulling.')
m('rhomboid-major','Rhomboid major','Musculus rhomboideus major',r'rhomboid major','shoulder','Between the spine and the shoulder blade.','Spinous processes of T2 to T5.','Medial border of the scapula below the spine.','Dorsal scapular nerve (C4, C5).','Pulls the scapula towards the spine and holds it against the chest wall.','Dorsal scapular artery.','Injury of the dorsal scapular nerve causes a winged shoulder blade that tilts slightly.')
m('rhomboid-minor','Rhomboid minor','Musculus rhomboideus minor',r'rhomboid minor','shoulder','Between the spine and the shoulder blade, above rhomboid major.','Lower ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7 and T1.','Medial border of the scapula at the root of the spine.','Dorsal scapular nerve (C4, C5).','Pulls the scapula towards the spine and holds it against the chest wall.','Dorsal scapular artery.','Strain between the shoulder blades is common with poor posture.')
m('levator-scapulae','Levator scapulae','Musculus levator scapulae',r'levator scapulae','shoulder','Side and back of the neck, running to the shoulder blade.','Transverse processes of C1 to C4.','Medial border of the scapula from the top to the root of the spine.','Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and cervical nerves C3, C4.','Lifts the scapula (shrugging) and tilts its lower angle towards the spine.','Dorsal scapular and transverse cervical arteries.','A frequent source of neck and shoulder tension.')
m('serratus-anterior','Serratus anterior','Musculus serratus anterior',r'serratus anterior','shoulder','Side of the chest wall under the armpit.','Outer surfaces of the upper eight or nine ribs.','Front of the medial border of the scapula.','Long thoracic nerve (C5, C6, C7).','Pulls the scapula forward around the chest wall and holds it flat. It also turns the scapula to raise the arm above shoulder height.','Lateral thoracic and thoracodorsal arteries.','Long thoracic nerve injury (for example in breast or axillary surgery) causes a winged scapula that sticks out when pushing against a wall.')
m('pectoralis-minor','Pectoralis minor','Musculus pectoralis minor',r'pectoralis minor','shoulder','Deep to pectoralis major, in the upper front chest.','Outer surfaces of ribs 3 to 5 near their cartilages.','Medial border and upper surface of the coracoid process of the scapula.','Medial pectoral nerve (C8, T1) and lateral pectoral nerve.','Pulls the scapula forward and down, and lifts the ribs when the scapula is fixed.','Thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries.','The axillary artery and brachial plexus pass behind it. Tightness can compress them (pectoralis minor syndrome).')
m('pectoralis-major','Pectoralis major','Musculus pectoralis major',r'(clavicular|sternocostal|abdominal) part of pectoralis major|pectoralis major','chest','Anterior chest wall.','Clavicular head: medial half of the clavicle. Sternocostal head: sternum and upper six costal cartilages. Abdominal part: upper rectus sheath.','Lateral lip of the bicipital groove of the humerus.','Lateral and medial pectoral nerves (C5 to T1).','Adducts and rotates the arm inward. The clavicular part flexes the arm; the sternocostal part extends it from a flexed position.','Pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery and internal thoracic artery.','Rupture of its tendon causes a loss of the front fold of the armpit. Absence (Poland syndrome) is a congenital condition.')
m('trapezius','Trapezius','Musculus trapezius',r'(ascending|transverse|descending) part of trapezius|trapezius','shoulder','Back of the neck and upper back, from the skull to T12.','Superior nuchal line, external occipital protuberance, ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7 to T12.','Lateral third of the clavicle, acromion and spine of the scapula.','Accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI) for movement; C3 and C4 for pain and position sense.','Elevates, retracts and rotates the scapula. The upper fibres shrug the shoulder; the lower fibres pull it down.','Transverse cervical artery and dorsal scapular artery.','Damage to the accessory nerve (neck surgery or lymph node biopsy) causes a drooping shoulder and weakness of shrugging.')
# ---------------- forearm ----------------
m('pronator-teres','Pronator teres','Musculus pronator teres',r'(humeral|ulnar) head of pronator teres|pronator teres','forearm','Front of the forearm, running obliquely from the inner elbow.','Humeral head: medial epicondyle. Ulnar head: coronoid process of the ulna.','Middle of the lateral surface of the radius.','Median nerve (C6, C7).','Turns the forearm so the palm faces down (pronation) and helps flex the elbow.','Ulnar and radial arteries.','The median nerve passes between its two heads and can be trapped there (pronator syndrome).')
m('flexor-carpi-radialis','Flexor carpi radialis','Musculus flexor carpi radialis',r'flexor carpi radialis','forearm','Front of the forearm, on the thumb side.','Medial epicondyle of the humerus (common flexor tendon).','Bases of the second and third metacarpal bones.','Median nerve (C6, C7).','Flexes and abducts the wrist (radial deviation).','Radial and ulnar arteries.','Its tendon is a landmark for the radial pulse and for the median nerve at the wrist.')
m('palmaris-longus','Palmaris longus','Musculus palmaris longus',r'palmaris longus','forearm','Front of the forearm, a slender muscle with a long tendon.','Medial epicondyle of the humerus.','Flexor retinaculum and palmar aponeurosis.','Median nerve (C7, C8).','Weakly flexes the wrist and tightens the palmar fascia.','Ulnar artery.','Absent in about 15 per cent of people. Its tendon is often used as a graft in hand surgery.')
m('flexor-carpi-ulnaris','Flexor carpi ulnaris','Musculus flexor carpi ulnaris',r'(humeral|ulnar) head of flexor carpi ulnaris|flexor carpi ulnaris','forearm','Front of the forearm on the little finger side.','Humeral head: medial epicondyle. Ulnar head: olecranon and the upper back of the ulna.','Pisiform bone, then hook of the hamate and the fifth metacarpal by ligaments.','Ulnar nerve (C7, C8).','Flexes and adducts the wrist (ulnar deviation).','Ulnar artery.','The ulnar nerve runs between its two heads in the cubital tunnel. Compression there causes numbness of the little finger.')
m('flexor-digitorum-superficialis','Flexor digitorum superficialis','Musculus flexor digitorum superficialis',r'(humeroulnar|radial) head of flexor digitorum superficialis|flexor digitorum superficialis','forearm','Middle layer of the front of the forearm.','Humeroulnar head: medial epicondyle, ulnar collateral ligament and coronoid process. Radial head: upper half of the anterior border of the radius.','Sides of the middle phalanges of the four fingers.','Median nerve (C7, C8, T1).','Flexes the middle joints of the fingers, then the knuckles and the wrist.','Ulnar artery.','Tendon injuries in the palm ("no man\'s land") need careful repair. The median nerve runs deep to the muscle.')
m('flexor-digitorum-profundus','Flexor digitorum profundus','Musculus flexor digitorum profundus',r'flexor digitorum profundus','forearm','Deep layer of the front of the forearm.','Upper three-quarters of the front and medial surfaces of the ulna and the interosseous membrane.','Bases of the distal phalanges of the four fingers.','Medial half (ring and little fingers): ulnar nerve. Lateral half (index and middle fingers): anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve (C8, T1).','Flexes the tip joints of the fingers. It is the only muscle that bends the end joint.','Anterior interosseous and ulnar arteries.','A torn tendon from a sudden pull on a gripped finger ("jersey finger") prevents bending the fingertip.')
m('flexor-pollicis-longus','Flexor pollicis longus','Musculus flexor pollicis longus',r'flexor pollicis longus','forearm','Deep front of the forearm on the thumb side.','Front of the radius and the adjacent interosseous membrane.','Base of the distal phalanx of the thumb.','Anterior interosseous nerve (median nerve, C8, T1).','Flexes the tip joint of the thumb.','Anterior interosseous and radial arteries.','Loss of thumb tip flexion with a pinch like an "OK sign" suggests an anterior interosseous nerve lesion.')
m('pronator-quadratus','Pronator quadratus','Musculus pronator quadratus',r'pronator quadratus','forearm','Front of the lower forearm, just above the wrist.','Distal quarter of the front of the ulna.','Distal quarter of the front of the radius.','Anterior interosseous nerve (C8, T1).','Pronates the forearm and holds the radius and ulna together at the wrist.','Anterior interosseous artery.','A fracture of the distal radius can displace or injure it.')
m('brachioradialis','Brachioradialis','Musculus brachioradialis',r'brachioradialis','forearm','Along the outer edge of the forearm.','Upper two-thirds of the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus.','Lateral side of the base of the radial styloid process.','Radial nerve (C5, C6).','Flexes the elbow, most strongly when the forearm is in a neutral (thumb up) position.','Radial recurrent artery.','The brachioradialis reflex tests C5 and C6. It is often spared in radial nerve injuries at the arm.')
m('extensor-carpi-radialis-longus','Extensor carpi radialis longus','Musculus extensor carpi radialis longus',r'extensor carpi radialis longus','forearm','Back of the forearm on the thumb side.','Lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus.','Dorsal base of the second metacarpal bone.','Radial nerve (C6, C7).','Extends and abducts the wrist.','Radial artery and radial recurrent artery.','Radial nerve palsy causes wrist drop because the wrist extensors lose their supply.')
m('extensor-carpi-radialis-brevis','Extensor carpi radialis brevis','Musculus extensor carpi radialis brevis',r'extensor carpi radialis brevis','forearm','Back of the forearm, next to the long radial extensor.','Lateral epicondyle of the humerus (common extensor tendon).','Dorsal base of the third metacarpal bone.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends and abducts the wrist and steadies it during gripping.','Radial artery.','The commonest site of tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis).')
m('extensor-digitorum','Extensor digitorum','Musculus extensor digitorum',r'extensor digitorum','forearm','Back of the forearm, the main finger extensor.','Lateral epicondyle of the humerus (common extensor tendon).','Extensor expansions of the four fingers, ending on the middle and distal phalanges.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends the fingers and the wrist.','Posterior interosseous artery.','Rupture of the extensor tendon over the finger causes a drooping fingertip ("mallet finger").')
m('extensor-digiti-minimi','Extensor digiti minimi','Musculus extensor digiti minimi',r'extensor digiti minimi','forearm','Back of the forearm on the little finger side.','Lateral epicondyle of the humerus (common extensor tendon).','Extensor expansion of the little finger.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends the little finger.','Posterior interosseous artery.','It lets the little finger extend on its own.')
m('supinator','Supinator','Musculus supinator',r'supinator','forearm','Deep, wrapping around the upper radius.','Lateral epicondyle of the humerus, the radial collateral ligament and the supinator crest of the ulna.','Upper third of the lateral, front and back surfaces of the radius.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C5, C6).','Turns the forearm so the palm faces up (supination).','Radial recurrent and posterior interosseous arteries.','The posterior interosseous nerve passes through it (arcade of Frohse). Compression there causes weak finger extension without sensory loss.')
m('abductor-pollicis-longus','Abductor pollicis longus','Musculus abductor pollicis longus',r'abductor pollicis longus','forearm','Back of the forearm, emerging near the wrist on the thumb side.','Back of the ulna, radius and interosseous membrane.','Base of the first metacarpal bone.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Abducts and extends the thumb.','Posterior interosseous artery.','With extensor pollicis brevis it forms the radial border of the anatomical snuffbox. De Quervain tenosynovitis affects both tendons.')
m('extensor-pollicis-brevis','Extensor pollicis brevis','Musculus extensor pollicis brevis',r'extensor pollicis brevis','forearm','Back of the forearm, running beside abductor pollicis longus.','Back of the radius and interosseous membrane.','Base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends the first joint of the thumb.','Posterior interosseous artery.','Involved in De Quervain tenosynovitis (pain at the thumb side of the wrist).')
m('extensor-pollicis-longus','Extensor pollicis longus','Musculus extensor pollicis longus',r'extensor pollicis longus','forearm','Back of the forearm; its tendon forms the ulnar border of the anatomical snuffbox.','Middle third of the back of the ulna and the interosseous membrane.','Base of the distal phalanx of the thumb.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends the tip of the thumb and helps abduct the wrist.','Posterior interosseous artery.','Its tendon can rupture after a wrist fracture, making it hard to lift the thumb.')
m('extensor-indicis','Extensor indicis','Musculus extensor indicis',r'extensor indicis','forearm','Deep back of the forearm.','Back of the ulna and interosseous membrane.','Extensor expansion of the index finger.','Posterior interosseous nerve (C7, C8).','Extends the index finger independently (as in pointing).','Posterior interosseous artery.','Its tendon is often used for tendon transfers.')
# ---------------- hand ----------------
m('abductor-pollicis-brevis','Abductor pollicis brevis','Musculus abductor pollicis brevis',r'abductor pollicis brevis','hand','Thenar eminence (thumb base).','Flexor retinaculum, scaphoid and trapezium.','Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.','Recurrent branch of the median nerve (C8, T1).','Abducts the thumb away from the palm.','Superficial palmar arch and radial artery.','Wasting of the thenar eminence is seen in severe carpal tunnel syndrome.')
m('opponens-pollicis','Opponens pollicis','Musculus opponens pollicis',r'opponens pollicis','hand','Deep in the thenar eminence.','Flexor retinaculum and trapezium.','Lateral half of the front of the first metacarpal.','Recurrent branch of the median nerve (C8, T1).','Brings the thumb across the palm to touch the fingertips.','Superficial palmar arch.','Loss of opposition is a key sign of median nerve injury at the wrist.')
m('flexor-pollicis-brevis','Flexor pollicis brevis','Musculus flexor pollicis brevis',r'(superficial|deep) head of flexor pollicis brevis|flexor pollicis brevis','hand','Thenar eminence.','Flexor retinaculum and trapezium (superficial head); trapezoid and capitate (deep head).','Base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.','Superficial head: median nerve. Deep head: ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Flexes the knuckle joint of the thumb.','Superficial palmar arch.','A dual nerve supply explains why thumb flexion is partly kept after one nerve injury.')
m('adductor-pollicis','Adductor pollicis','Musculus adductor pollicis',r'(oblique|transverse) head of adductor pollicis|adductor pollicis','hand','Deep in the web between thumb and index finger.','Oblique head: capitate and bases of the second and third metacarpals. Transverse head: third metacarpal shaft.','Medial side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Adducts the thumb towards the palm and gives pinch strength.','Deep palmar arch.','In ulnar nerve palsy the thumb flexes to compensate when pinching a sheet of paper (Froment sign).')
m('abductor-digiti-minimi-hand','Abductor digiti minimi (hand)','Musculus abductor digiti minimi manus',r'abductor digiti minimi of hand','hand','Hypothenar eminence (the pad below the little finger).','Pisiform bone and the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris.','Medial side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the little finger.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Abducts the little finger.','Ulnar artery and superficial palmar arch.','Wasting of the hypothenar eminence points to an ulnar nerve lesion.')
m('flexor-digiti-minimi-brevis-hand','Flexor digiti minimi brevis (hand)','Musculus flexor digiti minimi brevis manus',r'flexor digiti minimi brevis of hand','hand','Hypothenar eminence.','Hook of the hamate and flexor retinaculum.','Base of the proximal phalanx of the little finger.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Flexes the knuckle of the little finger.','Ulnar artery.','Weak in ulnar nerve injury.')
m('opponens-digiti-minimi-hand','Opponens digiti minimi (hand)','Musculus opponens digiti minimi manus',r'opponens digiti minimi of hand','hand','Deep in the hypothenar eminence.','Hook of the hamate and flexor retinaculum.','Medial border of the fifth metacarpal.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Draws the fifth metacarpal forward to cup the palm.','Ulnar artery.','Weak in ulnar nerve injury (loss of cupping the palm).')
m('lumbricals-hand','Lumbricals of the hand','Musculi lumbricales manus',r'set of lumbricals of hand','hand','Four small muscles in the palm, arising from the flexor tendons.','Tendons of flexor digitorum profundus in the palm.','Extensor expansions on the back of the four fingers.','First and second: median nerve. Third and fourth: deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8, T1).','Flex the knuckles and extend the finger joints at the same time.','Superficial and deep palmar arches.','In a claw hand (ulnar nerve palsy) the ring and little fingers are bent because the medial lumbricals are lost.')
m('dorsal-interossei-hand','Dorsal interossei of the hand','Musculi interossei dorsales manus',r'set of dorsal interossei of hand','hand','Four muscles between the metacarpal bones.','Adjacent sides of two metacarpal bones each (two heads).','Extensor expansions and bases of the proximal phalanges of the index, middle and ring fingers.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (T1).','Spread the fingers (abduction) and flex the knuckles.','Deep palmar arch and dorsal metacarpal arteries.','Wasting between the metacarpals is seen in ulnar nerve palsy and in diseases of the anterior horn cells.')
m('palmar-interossei-hand','Palmar interossei of the hand','Musculi interossei palmares manus',r'set of palmar interossei of hand','hand','Three small muscles on the palm side between the metacarpals.','Palmar sides of the second, fourth and fifth metacarpals.','Extensor expansions of the same fingers.','Deep branch of the ulnar nerve (T1).','Bring the fingers together (adduction).','Deep palmar arch.','Loss weakens finger adduction, which can be tested by gripping a card between the fingers.')
# ---------------- hip and thigh ----------------
m('gluteus-maximus','Gluteus maximus','Musculus gluteus maximus',r'gluteus maximus','hip','The largest and most superficial muscle of the buttock.','Gluteal surface of the ilium behind the posterior gluteal line, the sacrum and coccyx, and the sacrotuberous ligament.','Gluteal tuberosity of the femur (deep fibres) and the iliotibial tract (superficial fibres).','Inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Extends and rotates the thigh outward at the hip. It is used for climbing stairs, running and getting up from sitting.','Superior and inferior gluteal arteries.','Injury of the inferior gluteal nerve causes difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. It is a common site for deep injections only in its upper outer part.')
m('gluteus-medius','Gluteus medius','Musculus gluteus medius',r'gluteus medius','hip','Outer surface of the hip, deep to gluteus maximus.','Outer surface of the ilium between the anterior and posterior gluteal lines.','Lateral surface of the greater trochanter of the femur.','Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1).','Abducts the thigh and holds the pelvis level when standing on one leg.','Superior gluteal artery.','Weakness causes the pelvis to drop on the opposite side when walking (positive Trendelenburg sign).')
m('gluteus-minimus','Gluteus minimus','Musculus gluteus minimus',r'gluteus minimus','hip','Deepest of the three gluteal muscles.','Outer surface of the ilium between the anterior and inferior gluteal lines.','Anterior surface of the greater trochanter of the femur.','Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1).','Abducts and rotates the thigh inward; helps keep the pelvis level.','Superior gluteal artery.','Works with gluteus medius; both are involved in greater trochanteric pain syndrome.')
m('tensor-fasciae-latae','Tensor fasciae latae','Musculus tensor fasciae latae',r'tensor fasciae latae','hip','Upper outer thigh, in front of the hip.','Anterior superior iliac spine and the outer lip of the iliac crest.','Iliotibial tract, which ends on the lateral condyle of the tibia.','Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5).','Tenses the iliotibial tract, flexes and abducts the thigh and steadies the knee.','Superior gluteal artery and lateral circumflex femoral artery.','A tight iliotibial tract is linked to lateral knee pain in runners (iliotibial band syndrome).')
m('piriformis','Piriformis','Musculus piriformis',r'piriformis','hip','Deep buttock, running from the sacrum to the greater trochanter.','Front of the sacrum (S2 to S4) and the sacrotuberous ligament.','Upper border of the greater trochanter of the femur.','Nerve to piriformis (S1, S2).','Rotates the thigh outward when the hip is extended and abducts it when the hip is flexed.','Superior and inferior gluteal arteries.','The sciatic nerve passes close to it, below it in most people. Spasm may compress the nerve (piriformis syndrome).')
m('obturator-internus','Obturator internus','Musculus obturator internus',r'obturator internus','hip','Inside the pelvis, turning around the lesser sciatic notch to the hip.','Inner surface of the obturator membrane and the surrounding pubis and ischium.','Medial surface of the greater trochanter of the femur.','Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1).','Rotates the thigh outward and steadies the hip joint.','Obturator and internal pudendal arteries.','Forms part of the side wall of the pelvis and the ischioanal fossa.')
m('obturator-externus','Obturator externus','Musculus obturator externus',r'obturator externus','hip','Deep, at the front and inner side of the hip.','Outer surface of the obturator membrane and the pubic and ischial rami.','Trochanteric fossa of the femur.','Obturator nerve (L3, L4).','Rotates the thigh outward and steadies the hip.','Obturator and medial circumflex femoral arteries.','The medial circumflex artery lies beside it and supplies the femoral head.')
m('gemellus-superior','Gemellus superior','Musculus gemellus superior',r'gemellus superior','hip','Small muscle above the obturator internus tendon.','Ischial spine.','Medial surface of the greater trochanter with the obturator internus tendon.','Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1).','Rotates the thigh outward.','Inferior gluteal artery.','Part of the group of small outward rotators deep to gluteus maximus.')
m('gemellus-inferior','Gemellus inferior','Musculus gemellus inferior',r'gemellus inferior','hip','Small muscle below the obturator internus tendon.','Ischial tuberosity.','Medial surface of the greater trochanter with the obturator internus tendon.','Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, L5, S1).','Rotates the thigh outward.','Inferior gluteal artery.','Part of the group of small outward rotators.')
m('quadratus-femoris','Quadratus femoris','Musculus quadratus femoris',r'quadratus femoris','hip','Deep, below the gemelli, at the back of the hip.','Lateral border of the ischial tuberosity.','Quadrate tubercle on the intertrochanteric crest of the femur.','Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, L5, S1).','Rotates the thigh outward and steadies the hip joint.','Medial circumflex femoral artery.','The medial circumflex artery runs deep to it and is at risk in hip fractures.')
m('iliacus','Iliacus','Musculus iliacus',r'iliacus','hip','Inside the pelvis, lining the iliac fossa.','Iliac fossa and the ala of the sacrum.','Lesser trochanter of the femur, joining the tendon of psoas major.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3).','Flexes the thigh at the hip (with psoas major) and steadies the trunk.','Iliolumbar artery and deep circumflex iliac artery.','An iliacus abscess or bleed can compress the femoral nerve.')
m('psoas-major','Psoas major','Musculus psoas major',r'psoas major','hip','Along the side of the lumbar spine, passing over the pelvic brim.','Sides of the bodies of T12 to L5 and their discs, and the transverse processes of the lumbar vertebrae.','Lesser trochanter of the femur.','Ventral rami of L1 to L3 (lumbar plexus).','Flexes the thigh at the hip and bends the lumbar spine sideways.','Lumbar branches of the abdominal aorta and iliolumbar artery.','A tuberculous abscess of the spine can track down the sheath of psoas to the groin. Pain on extending the hip suggests psoas irritation (as in appendicitis).')
m('sartorius','Sartorius','Musculus sartorius',r'sartorius','thigh','The longest muscle, crossing the front of the thigh diagonally.','Anterior superior iliac spine.','Upper medial surface of the tibia (pes anserinus).','Femoral nerve (L2, L3).','Flexes, abducts and rotates the thigh outward and flexes the knee, as in sitting cross-legged.','Femoral artery (muscular branches).','Forms the roof of the adductor canal and the lateral border of the femoral triangle.')
m('rectus-femoris','Rectus femoris','Musculus rectus femoris',r'rectus femoris','thigh','Front of the thigh.','Anterior inferior iliac spine and the ilium above the acetabulum.','Base of the patella, then the tibial tuberosity via the patellar ligament.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).','Extends the knee and flexes the hip.','Lateral circumflex femoral artery.','A common site of sprints and kicking strains.')
m('vastus-lateralis','Vastus lateralis','Musculus vastus lateralis',r'vastus lateralis','thigh','Outer front of the thigh, the largest part of quadriceps.','Greater trochanter, the intertrochanteric line and the lateral lip of the linea aspera.','Base and lateral border of the patella, then the tibial tuberosity.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).','Extends the knee.','Lateral circumflex femoral artery.','A common site for intramuscular injections in infants.')
m('vastus-medialis','Vastus medialis','Musculus vastus medialis',r'vastus medialis','thigh','Inner front of the thigh, with its fibres running low towards the knee.','Intertrochanteric line, the spiral line and the medial lip of the linea aspera.','Medial border of the patella, then the tibial tuberosity.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).','Extends the knee and keeps the patella from sliding outward.','Femoral and deep femoral arteries.','Weakness of its lower fibres is linked to kneecap pain and patellar instability.')
m('vastus-intermedius','Vastus intermedius','Musculus vastus intermedius',r'vastus intermedius','thigh','Deep to rectus femoris, on the front of the femur.','Front and sides of the upper two-thirds of the femoral shaft.','Base of the patella, then the tibial tuberosity.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).','Extends the knee.','Lateral circumflex femoral artery.','The quadriceps tendon formed with the other three parts is tested by the knee-jerk reflex (L3, L4).')
m('pectineus','Pectineus','Musculus pectineus',r'pectineus','thigh','Upper inner thigh, forming part of the floor of the femoral triangle.','Pecten pubis (superior ramus of the pubis).','Pectineal line of the femur, behind the lesser trochanter.','Femoral nerve (L2, L3) and often the obturator nerve.','Flexes and adducts the thigh.','Medial circumflex femoral and obturator arteries.','A groin strain can involve it. Its nerve supply from two nerves is a classic exam point.')
m('adductor-longus','Adductor longus','Musculus adductor longus',r'adductor longus','thigh','Inner thigh, the most superficial adductor.','Front of the pubis, below the pubic crest.','Middle third of the linea aspera of the femur.','Obturator nerve (L2, L3, L4).','Adducts the thigh.','Deep femoral artery (perforating branches).','The most commonly strained muscle in a groin pull ("gracilis and adductor strains" in footballers).')
m('adductor-brevis','Adductor brevis','Musculus adductor brevis',r'adductor brevis','thigh','Inner thigh, deep to pectineus and adductor longus.','Body and inferior ramus of the pubis.','Upper part of the linea aspera and the pectineal line of the femur.','Obturator nerve (L2, L3).','Adducts the thigh and helps flex it.','Deep femoral artery.','Separates the anterior and posterior branches of the obturator nerve.')
m('adductor-magnus','Adductor magnus','Musculus adductor magnus',r'adductor magnus|adductor minimus','thigh','The largest muscle of the inner thigh.','Inferior ramus of the pubis, ramus of the ischium and the ischial tuberosity.','Gluteal tuberosity, linea aspera and the adductor tubercle of the femur.','Obturator nerve (adductor part) and the tibial part of the sciatic nerve (hamstring part), L2 to L4.','Adducts the thigh; the hamstring part extends the hip.','Deep femoral artery and obturator artery.','Its adductor hiatus lets the femoral vessels pass to the back of the knee.')
m('gracilis','Gracilis','Musculus gracilis',r'gracilis','thigh','The long, thin muscle on the inner side of the thigh.','Body and inferior ramus of the pubis.','Upper medial surface of the tibia (pes anserinus).','Obturator nerve (L2, L3).','Adducts the thigh and flexes and rotates the leg inward at the knee.','Medial circumflex femoral artery.','Often used as a flap in reconstruction and for transfers in hand and pelvic surgery.')
m('semitendinosus','Semitendinosus','Musculus semitendinosus',r'semitendinosus','thigh','Back of the thigh, a hamstring muscle with a long tendon.','Ischial tuberosity.','Upper medial surface of the tibia (pes anserinus).','Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Extends the hip and flexes the knee; rotates the leg inward.','Perforating branches of the deep femoral artery.','Its tendon is a graft source for anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction.')
m('semimembranosus','Semimembranosus','Musculus semimembranosus',r'semimembranosus','thigh','Back of the thigh, deep to semitendinosus.','Ischial tuberosity.','Posterior surface of the medial tibial condyle.','Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Extends the hip and flexes the knee; rotates the leg inward.','Perforating branches of the deep femoral artery.','A fluid-filled swelling behind the knee (Baker cyst) often arises between its tendon and the gastrocnemius.')
m('biceps-femoris','Biceps femoris','Musculus biceps femoris',r'(long|short) head of biceps femoris|biceps femoris','thigh','Outer back of the thigh, a hamstring muscle.','Long head: ischial tuberosity. Short head: lateral lip of the linea aspera.','Head of the fibula and the lateral condyle of the tibia.','Long head: tibial part of the sciatic nerve. Short head: common fibular part (L5, S1, S2).','Flexes the knee and extends the hip; rotates the leg outward.','Perforating branches of the deep femoral artery.','The common fibular nerve runs along its tendon. Hamstring strains are common in sprinters.')
# ---------------- leg ----------------
m('gastrocnemius','Gastrocnemius','Musculus gastrocnemius',r'(medial|lateral) head of gastrocnemius|gastrocnemius','leg','The calf, forming the shape of the back of the leg.','Medial head: above the medial condyle of the femur. Lateral head: lateral condyle of the femur.','Calcaneus (heel bone) via the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon.','Tibial nerve (S1, S2).','Plantar-flexes the foot and flexes the knee.','Sural arteries (from the popliteal artery).','Rupture of the calcaneal tendon causes a gap above the heel and weak push-off.')
m('soleus','Soleus','Musculus soleus',r'soleus','leg','Deep to gastrocnemius in the calf.','Back of the head and upper shaft of the fibula, and the soleal line of the tibia.','Calcaneus via the calcaneal tendon.','Tibial nerve (S1, S2).','Plantar-flexes the foot. It is vital for standing and walking, and acts as a "peripheral heart" by pumping venous blood up the leg.','Posterior tibial and fibular arteries.','Deep vein thrombosis often starts in the soleal veins.')
m('plantaris','Plantaris','Musculus plantaris',r'plantaris','leg','Thin muscle between gastrocnemius and soleus.','Lateral supracondylar line of the femur.','Calcaneus, medial to the calcaneal tendon.','Tibial nerve (S1, S2).','Weakly assists plantar flexion and knee flexion.','Popliteal and sural arteries.','Its long tendon can be mistaken for a nerve and is used as a graft.')
m('calcaneal-tendon','Calcaneal (Achilles) tendon','Tendo calcaneus',r'calcaneal tendon','leg','Back of the ankle, the thickest tendon of the body.','Joined tendon of gastrocnemius and soleus.','Back of the calcaneus (heel bone).','Tibial nerve (S1, S2), through the calf muscles.','Transmits calf muscle force to the heel for push-off.','Posterior tibial and fibular arteries.','Rupture is common in middle-aged athletes. A positive Thompson test (no foot movement when the calf is squeezed) confirms it.')
m('popliteus','Popliteus','Musculus popliteus',r'popliteus','leg','Behind the knee, deep.','Lateral condyle of the femur and the lateral meniscus.','Posterior surface of the tibia above the soleal line.','Tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1).','Unlocks the knee at the start of flexion by rotating the femur outward on the tibia.','Inferior medial and lateral genicular arteries.','Injury is linked to posterolateral corner damage of the knee.')
m('tibialis-anterior','Tibialis anterior','Musculus tibialis anterior',r'tibialis anterior','leg','Front of the leg, beside the shin.','Upper two-thirds of the lateral surface of the tibia and the interosseous membrane.','Medial cuneiform bone and the base of the first metatarsal.','Deep fibular nerve (L4, L5).','Dorsiflexes the foot and inverts it.','Anterior tibial artery.','Weakness causes foot drop. Shin splints often involve this muscle.')
m('tibialis-posterior','Tibialis posterior','Musculus tibialis posterior',r'tibialis posterior','leg','Deep back of the leg.','Interosseous membrane and the back of the tibia and fibula.','Navicular tuberosity and most of the tarsal bones.','Tibial nerve (L4, L5).','Inverts the foot and plantar-flexes it; supports the medial arch.','Posterior tibial and fibular arteries.','Tendon dysfunction leads to an acquired flat foot.')
m('extensor-digitorum-longus','Extensor digitorum longus','Musculus extensor digitorum longus',r'extensor digitorum longus','leg','Front and outer leg.','Lateral condyle of the tibia, upper fibula and interosseous membrane.','Middle and distal phalanges of the four lateral toes.','Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1).','Extends the toes and dorsiflexes the foot.','Anterior tibial artery.','Compartment syndrome of the front of the leg can ischaemically damage it.')
m('extensor-hallucis-longus','Extensor hallucis longus','Musculus extensor hallucis longus',r'extensor hallucis longus','leg','Front of the leg, between tibialis anterior and extensor digitorum longus.','Middle of the front of the fibula and the interosseous membrane.','Base of the distal phalanx of the big toe.','Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1).','Extends the big toe and dorsiflexes the foot.','Anterior tibial artery.','Its tendon is a landmark for the dorsalis pedis pulse.')
m('fibularis-longus','Fibularis longus','Musculus fibularis longus',r'fibularis longus','leg','Outer leg.','Head and upper two-thirds of the lateral surface of the fibula.','Medial cuneiform bone and base of the first metatarsal.','Superficial fibular nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Everts the foot and plantar-flexes it; supports the arches.','Fibular and anterior tibial arteries.','Common fibular nerve injury at the fibular neck weakens eversion and dorsiflexion.')
m('fibularis-brevis','Fibularis brevis','Musculus fibularis brevis',r'fibularis brevis','leg','Outer leg, deep to fibularis longus.','Lower two-thirds of the lateral surface of the fibula.','Tuberosity of the base of the fifth metatarsal.','Superficial fibular nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Everts the foot and weakly plantar-flexes it.','Fibular artery.','Its tendon can be injured in ankle sprains; avulsion fracture of the fifth metatarsal base occurs with sudden inversion.')
m('fibularis-tertius','Fibularis tertius','Musculus fibularis tertius',r'fibularis tertius','leg','Front and outer lower leg, part of extensor digitorum longus.','Distal third of the front of the fibula.','Dorsal surface of the base of the fifth metatarsal.','Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1).','Dorsiflexes and everts the foot.','Anterior tibial artery.','Absent in about 10 per cent of people.')
m('flexor-digitorum-longus','Flexor digitorum longus','Musculus flexor digitorum longus',r'flexor digitorum longus','leg','Deep back of the leg, inner side.','Medial part of the back of the tibia below the soleal line.','Bases of the distal phalanges of the four lateral toes.','Tibial nerve (L5, S1, S2).','Flexes the toes and helps plantar-flex the foot.','Posterior tibial artery.','Its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus in the tarsal tunnel.')
m('flexor-hallucis-longus','Flexor hallucis longus','Musculus flexor hallucis longus',r'flexor hallucis longus','leg','Deep back of the leg, outer side.','Lower two-thirds of the back of the fibula.','Base of the distal phalanx of the big toe.','Tibial nerve (S1, S2).','Flexes the big toe and gives the main push-off in walking.','Fibular artery.','Tenosynovitis is common in dancers.')
# ---------------- foot ----------------
m('extensor-digitorum-brevis','Extensor digitorum brevis','Musculus extensor digitorum brevis',r'extensor digitorum brevis|extensor hallucis brevis','foot','Top of the foot, in front of the ankle.','Upper and lateral surface of the calcaneus.','Extensor expansions of the first to fourth toes (the part to the big toe is extensor hallucis brevis).','Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1).','Extends the toes.','Dorsalis pedis artery.','Swelling over the sinus tarsi should not be mistaken for a lump; the muscle can bruise in ankle sprains.')
m('abductor-hallucis','Abductor hallucis','Musculus abductor hallucis',r'abductor hallucis','foot','Inner edge of the sole.','Medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity and flexor retinaculum.','Medial side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the big toe.','Medial plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Abducts and flexes the big toe; supports the medial arch.','Medial plantar artery.','Tight or weak muscle is linked to a bunion (hallux valgus).')
m('flexor-digitorum-brevis','Flexor digitorum brevis','Musculus flexor digitorum brevis',r'flexor digitorum brevis','foot','Middle of the sole, just under the plantar aponeurosis.','Medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity and plantar aponeurosis.','Sides of the middle phalanges of the four lateral toes.','Medial plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Flexes the middle joints of the lateral four toes and supports the arch.','Medial and lateral plantar arteries.','Plantar fasciitis pain at the heel arises close to its origin.')
m('abductor-digiti-minimi-foot','Abductor digiti minimi (foot)','Musculus abductor digiti minimi pedis',r'abductor digiti minimi of foot','foot','Outer edge of the sole.','Medial and lateral processes of the calcaneal tuberosity.','Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the little toe.','Lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Abducts and flexes the little toe.','Lateral plantar artery.','Can be compressed in tight shoes.')
m('flexor-accessorius','Quadratus plantae (flexor accessorius)','Musculus quadratus plantae',r'flexor accessorius','foot','Deep in the sole.','Medial and lateral surfaces of the calcaneus.','Tendon of flexor digitorum longus.','Lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Straightens the pull of flexor digitorum longus so the toes flex directly.','Lateral plantar artery.','Weakness gives a less effective grip of the toes.')
m('flexor-hallucis-brevis','Flexor hallucis brevis','Musculus flexor hallucis brevis',r'(medial|lateral) head of flexor hallucis brevis|flexor hallucis brevis','foot','Under the first metatarsal.','Cuboid and lateral cuneiform bones.','Both sides of the base of the proximal phalanx of the big toe.','Medial plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Flexes the big toe joint. The two sesamoid bones lie in its tendons.','Medial plantar artery.','Sesamoiditis causes pain under the ball of the big toe.')
m('adductor-hallucis','Adductor hallucis','Musculus adductor hallucis',r'(oblique|transverse) head of adductor hallucis|adductor hallucis','foot','Deep in the forefoot of the sole.','Oblique head: bases of the second to fourth metatarsals. Transverse head: plantar ligaments of the third to fifth toes.','Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the big toe.','Lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Adducts the big toe and supports the transverse arch.','Lateral plantar artery and plantar arch.','Helps hold the big toe against hallux valgus drift.')
m('flexor-digiti-minimi-brevis-foot','Flexor digiti minimi brevis (foot)','Musculus flexor digiti minimi brevis pedis',r'flexor digiti minimi brevis of foot|opponens digiti minimi of foot','foot','Outer sole, beside the fifth metatarsal.','Base of the fifth metatarsal and the long plantar ligament.','Base of the proximal phalanx of the little toe.','Lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2).','Flexes the little toe.','Lateral plantar artery.','Small, with little clinical importance on its own.')
m('lumbricals-foot','Lumbricals of the foot','Musculi lumbricales pedis',r'(first|second|third|fourth) lumbrical of foot','foot','Four small muscles in the sole.','Tendons of flexor digitorum longus.','Extensor expansions on the first four toes.','First: medial plantar nerve. Others: lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3).','Flex the toe knuckles and extend the toe tips.','Plantar arch.','Wasting contributes to clawing of the toes in neuropathy.')
m('dorsal-interossei-foot','Dorsal interossei of the foot','Musculi interossei dorsales pedis',r'set of dorsal interossei of foot','foot','Between the metatarsal bones.','Adjacent sides of two metatarsal bones each.','Extensor expansions and proximal phalanges of the second to fourth toes.','Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3).','Spread the toes and flex the toe knuckles.','Dorsal metatarsal and plantar arteries.','Weakness gives claw toes in diabetic neuropathy.')
HAND_BONES=r'(carpal|capitate|hamate|lunate|pisiform|scaphoid|trapezium|trapezoid|triquetral|metacarpal|phalanx of.*hand|phalanx of hand)'
# ---------------- trunk, neck, head and pelvic floor ----------------
m('rectus-abdominis','Rectus abdominis','Musculus rectus abdominis',r'rectus abdominis','abdomen','Front of the abdomen, either side of the midline.','Pubic crest and pubic symphysis.','Xiphoid process and the costal cartilages of ribs 5 to 7.','Ventral rami of T7 to T12 (intercostal and subcostal nerves).','Flexes the trunk, tenses the abdominal wall and raises abdominal pressure.','Superior and inferior epigastric arteries.','Divarication of the two halves (diastasis recti) occurs after pregnancy. Epigastric vessels run behind it.')
m('external-oblique','External oblique','Musculus obliquus externus abdominis',r'external oblique','abdomen','Side and front of the abdomen, the most superficial lateral muscle.','Outer surfaces of the lower eight ribs.','Linea alba, pubic tubercle and the anterior half of the iliac crest.','Ventral rami of T7 to T12 and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves.','Flexes and twists the trunk to the opposite side, and compresses the abdomen.','Lower posterior intercostal, subcostal and deep circumflex iliac arteries.','Its aponeurosis forms the inguinal ligament. Weakness at the groin allows inguinal hernia.')
m('internal-oblique','Internal oblique','Musculus obliquus internus abdominis',r'internal oblique','abdomen','Deep to the external oblique on the side of the abdomen.','Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of the iliac crest and the lateral inguinal ligament.','Lower three or four ribs, linea alba and pubic crest (conjoint tendon).','Ventral rami of T7 to L1, including the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves.','Flexes and twists the trunk to the same side and compresses the abdomen.','Deep circumflex iliac and inferior epigastric arteries.','Its lower fibres form the conjoint tendon and the roof of the inguinal canal.')
m('transversus-abdominis','Transversus abdominis','Musculus transversus abdominis',r'transversus abdominis','abdomen','Deepest layer of the side abdominal wall.','Inner surfaces of the lower six costal cartilages, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and inguinal ligament.','Linea alba and pubic crest.','Ventral rami of T7 to L1.','Compresses the abdomen and stabilises the lower back.','Deep circumflex iliac and inferior epigastric arteries.','The "core" stabiliser. The TAP block anaesthetises nerves in the plane in front of it.')
m('quadratus-lumborum','Quadratus lumborum','Musculus quadratus lumborum',r'quadratus lumborum','abdomen','Back wall of the abdomen, beside the lumbar spine.','Iliac crest and iliolumbar ligament.','Twelfth rib and the transverse processes of L1 to L4.','Ventral rami of T12 to L4.','Bends the trunk sideways and fixes the twelfth rib in breathing.','Lumbar and subcostal arteries.','A common source of low back pain. The kidneys lie in front of it.')
m('erector-spinae','Erector spinae (iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis)','Musculus erector spinae',r'(iliocostalis|longissimus|spinalis) (capitis|cervicis|lumborum|thoracis)','back','Long columns on both sides of the spine.','Sacrum, iliac crest and spinous processes of the lumbar vertebrae (common tendon).','Ribs, transverse and spinous processes and the skull base (mastoid process).','Dorsal rami of spinal nerves.','Extends and straightens the spine and bends it sideways.','Posterior intercostal and lumbar arteries.','Strain gives the commonest form of low back pain. Spasm is felt as a tight band beside the spine.')
m('multifidus','Multifidus','Musculus multifidus',r'multifidus','back','Deep muscle along the spine, in the groove between spinous and transverse processes.','Sacrum, lumbar mammillary processes and thoracic transverse processes.','Spinous processes of the vertebrae two to four levels higher.','Dorsal rami of spinal nerves.','Stabilises the spine and extends and rotates it slightly.','Dorsal branches of the segmental arteries.','Wasting is linked to chronic low back pain.')
m('semispinalis','Semispinalis','Musculus semispinalis',r'semispinalis (capitis|cervicis|thoracis)','back','Deep muscle of the back of the neck and upper back.','Transverse processes of C4 to T12.','Occipital bone and spinous processes of the vertebrae above.','Dorsal rami of cervical and thoracic nerves.','Extends and rotates the head and spine.','Occipital and deep cervical arteries.','Weak in whiplash-type neck pain.')
m('splenius','Splenius capitis and cervicis','Musculus splenius',r'splenius (capitis|cervicis)','back','Back of the neck, under trapezius.','Ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7 to T6.','Mastoid process and the transverse processes of C1 to C3.','Dorsal rami of cervical nerves.','Extends and rotates the head and neck.','Occipital and transverse cervical arteries.','Tension here causes neck stiffness and headache.')
m('serratus-posterior-superior','Serratus posterior superior','Musculus serratus posterior superior',r'serratus posterior superior','back','Upper back, deep to the rhomboids.','Ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7 to T3.','Upper borders of ribs 2 to 5.','Ventral rami of T1 to T4.','Raises the ribs slightly in inspiration.','Posterior intercostal arteries.','A minor accessory muscle of breathing.')
m('serratus-posterior-inferior','Serratus posterior inferior','Musculus serratus posterior inferior',r'serratus posterior inferior','back','Lower back, deep to latissimus dorsi.','Spinous processes of T11 to L2.','Lower borders of ribs 9 to 12.','Ventral rami of T9 to T12.','Pulls the lower ribs down and back in expiration.','Posterior intercostal and lumbar arteries.','A minor muscle of breathing.')
m('transversus-thoracis','Transversus thoracis','Musculus transversus thoracis',r'transversus thoracis','abdomen','Inner surface of the front chest wall.','Back of the lower sternum and xiphoid process.','Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2 to 6.','Intercostal nerves (T2 to T6).','Depresses the costal cartilages in expiration.','Internal thoracic artery.','Lies behind the sternum; the internal thoracic vessels run in front of it.')
m('sternocleidomastoid','Sternocleidomastoid','Musculus sternocleidomastoideus',r'sternocleidomastoid','neck','Side of the neck, running from behind the ear to the collarbone.','Manubrium of the sternum and the medial third of the clavicle.','Mastoid process and the lateral superior nuchal line.','Accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI) and cervical nerves C2, C3.','Turns the head to the opposite side and tilts it to the same side; both together flex the neck.','Occipital and superior thyroid arteries.','Congenital torticollis comes from shortening or fibrosis of this muscle in infants. Accessory nerve injury causes weakness.')
m('scalene-muscles','Scalene muscles','Musculi scaleni',r'scalenus (anterior|medius|posterior)','neck','Side of the neck, from the cervical spine to the first two ribs.','Transverse processes of C2 to C7.','First rib (anterior and middle scalenes) and second rib (posterior scalene).','Ventral rami of C3 to C8.','Raise the first and second ribs in breathing and bend the neck sideways.','Ascending cervical and transverse cervical arteries.','The brachial plexus and subclavian artery pass between the anterior and middle scalenes. Compression here causes thoracic outlet syndrome.')
m('platysma','Platysma','Musculus platysma',r'platysma','neck','Thin sheet just under the skin of the neck.','Fascia over the upper chest and shoulder.','Lower border of the mandible and the skin of the lower face.','Cervical branch of the facial nerve.','Tightens the skin of the neck and pulls the corner of the mouth down.','Facial and submental arteries.','Facial nerve palsy can cause loss of its tension.')
m('subclavius','Subclavius','Musculus subclavius',r'subclavius','shoulder','Small muscle under the clavicle.','First rib and its costal cartilage.','Groove on the underside of the clavicle.','Nerve to subclavius (C5, C6).','Draws the clavicle down and steadies the sternoclavicular joint.','Thoracoacromial artery.','Lies in front of the brachial plexus and subclavian vessels and protects them in clavicle fractures.')
m('longus-colli','Longus colli','Musculus longus colli',r'(inferior oblique|superior oblique|vertical intermediate) part of longus colli','neck','Front of the cervical spine.','Bodies of the upper thoracic and lower cervical vertebrae.','Atlas and bodies of the upper cervical vertebrae.','Ventral rami of C2 to C6.','Flexes the neck.','Inferior thyroid and ascending pharyngeal arteries.','Injury (whiplash) can swell the retropharyngeal space.')
m('longus-capitis','Longus capitis','Musculus longus capitis',r'longus capitis','neck','Front of the upper cervical spine.','Transverse processes of C3 to C6.','Basilar part of the occipital bone.','Ventral rami of C1 to C3.','Flexes the head and neck.','Ascending cervical artery.','A deep neck flexor weakened in neck pain.')
m('masseter','Masseter','Musculus masseter',r'(superficial|deep) part of masseter','head','Side of the cheek, over the angle of the jaw.','Zygomatic arch.','Lateral surface of the ramus and angle of the mandible.','Mandibular nerve (V3) through the masseteric nerve.','Closes the jaw and clenches the teeth.','Masseteric branch of the maxillary artery and facial artery.','Enlarged by teeth grinding (bruxism). Pain here is common in temporomandibular joint disorders.')
m('temporalis','Temporalis','Musculus temporalis',r'temporalis','head','Side of the head, in the temporal fossa.','Temporal fossa and the temporal fascia.','Coronoid process and the anterior border of the ramus of the mandible.','Deep temporal branches of the mandibular nerve (V3).','Closes the jaw; the posterior fibres retract the jaw.','Deep temporal arteries.','Pain in the temple can mean tension headache; temporal arteritis affects the artery above it.')
m('medial-pterygoid','Medial pterygoid','Musculus pterygoideus medialis',r'medial pterygoid','head','Deep to the ramus of the mandible.','Medial surface of the lateral pterygoid plate and the pyramidal process of the palatine bone.','Medial surface of the angle of the mandible.','Medial pterygoid nerve from the mandibular nerve (V3).','Closes the jaw and moves it sideways.','Pterygoid branches of the maxillary artery.','Spasm limits jaw opening (trismus) after a wisdom tooth operation.')
m('lateral-pterygoid','Lateral pterygoid','Musculus pterygoideus lateralis',r'(upper|lower) head of lateral pterygoid','head','Deep in the infratemporal fossa.','Upper head: infratemporal surface of the greater wing of the sphenoid. Lower head: lateral surface of the lateral pterygoid plate.','Neck of the mandible and the articular disc of the temporomandibular joint.','Lateral pterygoid nerve from the mandibular nerve (V3).','Opens the jaw by pulling the mandible forward and moves it side to side.','Pterygoid branches of the maxillary artery.','Dysfunction contributes to clicking of the jaw joint.')
m('digastric','Digastric','Musculus digastricus',r'(anterior|posterior) belly of digastric','neck','Below the jaw, two bellies joined by an intermediate tendon at the hyoid.','Anterior belly: digastric fossa of the mandible. Posterior belly: mastoid notch.','Intermediate tendon attached to the hyoid bone.','Anterior belly: nerve to mylohyoid (V3). Posterior belly: facial nerve.','Opens the jaw and raises the hyoid in swallowing.','Facial, lingual and occipital arteries.','Landmark that divides the neck into triangles.')
m('mylohyoid','Mylohyoid','Musculus mylohyoideus',r'mylohyoid','neck','Floor of the mouth.','Mylohyoid line of the mandible.','Hyoid bone and median raphe.','Nerve to mylohyoid (V3).','Raises the floor of the mouth and the hyoid in swallowing.','Submental and sublingual arteries.','Forms the muscular floor of the mouth; infection spreads above or below it in Ludwig angina.')
m('geniohyoid','Geniohyoid','Musculus geniohyoideus',r'geniohyoid','neck','Above the mylohyoid in the floor of the mouth.','Inferior mental spine of the mandible.','Body of the hyoid bone.','C1 fibres carried by the hypoglossal nerve.','Pulls the hyoid forward and up.','Sublingual artery.','Helps open the jaw and keeps the airway open in sleep.')
m('stylohyoid','Stylohyoid','Musculus stylohyoideus',r'stylohyoid','neck','Runs along the digastric from the skull to the hyoid.','Styloid process of the temporal bone.','Body of the hyoid bone.','Facial nerve.','Pulls the hyoid up and back in swallowing.','Facial and occipital arteries.','An elongated styloid process can cause throat pain (Eagle syndrome).')
m('omohyoid','Omohyoid','Musculus omohyoideus',r'omohyoid','neck','Side of the neck, from the shoulder blade to the hyoid.','Superior border of the scapula.','Lower border of the hyoid bone.','Ansa cervicalis (C1 to C3).','Depresses and steadies the hyoid.','Superior and inferior thyroid arteries.','Landmark that divides the posterior triangle of the neck.')
m('sternohyoid','Sternohyoid','Musculus sternohyoideus',r'sternohyoid','neck','Front of the neck, over the thyroid gland.','Manubrium and the medial end of the clavicle.','Lower border of the hyoid bone.','Ansa cervicalis (C1 to C3).','Depresses the hyoid after swallowing.','Superior thyroid artery.','One of the "strap muscles" divided in thyroid surgery.')
m('sternothyroid','Sternothyroid','Musculus sternothyroideus',r'sternothyroid','neck','Front of the neck, deep to sternohyoid.','Back of the manubrium.','Oblique line of the thyroid cartilage.','Ansa cervicalis (C1 to C3).','Pulls the larynx down.','Superior thyroid artery.','Lies directly in front of the thyroid gland.')
m('thyrohyoid','Thyrohyoid','Musculus thyrohyoideus',r'thyrohyoid','neck','Front of the neck, above the thyroid cartilage.','Oblique line of the thyroid cartilage.','Greater horn and body of the hyoid bone.','C1 fibres carried by the hypoglossal nerve.','Raises the larynx and lowers the hyoid.','Superior laryngeal artery.','Helps close the airway in swallowing.')
m('buccinator','Buccinator','Musculus buccinator',r'buccinator','head','The muscle of the cheek.','Outer surfaces of the maxilla and mandible near the molars, and the pterygomandibular raphe.','Orbicularis oris and the angle of the mouth.','Buccal branches of the facial nerve.','Presses the cheek against the teeth in chewing and blowing.','Facial and buccal arteries.','Facial palsy lets food collect in the cheek.')
m('pelvic-floor-muscles','Levator ani and coccygeus','Musculi levator ani et coccygeus',r'iliococcygeus|pubococcygeus|coccygeus','pelvis','Floor of the pelvis, forming a sling.','Pubic bone, tendinous arch of levator ani and ischial spine.','Perineal body, anococcygeal ligament and coccyx.','Nerve to levator ani (S3, S4) and the pudendal nerve.','Supports the pelvic organs and keeps urinary and faecal continence.','Inferior gluteal and internal pudendal arteries.','Childbirth can damage it, leading to prolapse and stress incontinence.')
